<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="inositol pyrophosphates biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6369" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; In the early 1990s, two groups working independently discovered a novel subgroup of the inositol phosphate signaling family in which diphosphate groups are added to |FRAME: CPD-1107| and |FRAME: MI-HEXAKISPHOSPHATE| |CITS: [8440693][8382679]|, forming compounds that are now known as inositol pyrophosphates or diphosphoinositol polyphosphates |CITS: [16429326]|. These molecules have been reported to be involved in the regulation of vesicle trafficking |CITS: [12391334]|, transcription |CITS: [12828642]|, chemotaxis |CITS: [13678580]|, telomere maintenance |CITS: [15561716][15665079]|, apoptosis |CITS: [15533939][11337497]|, and DNA repair |CITS: [11851397]|, and to mediate environmental stress responses |CITS:[11956213][15316027][15342793][15936174]|. The concentration of the molecules appear to be tightly regulated with an extremely rapid turnover |CITS: [19326391]|. By inhibiting the enzymes that remove the pyrophosphate groups (encoded by |FRAME: HS03602|, |FRAME: HS10696|, |FRAME: HS04604| and |FRAME: HS05381|) it was possible to calculate that their cellular pools were normally turning over at least 10 times every 40 min |CITS: [8343137]|. The pathway that leads to the biosynthesis of inositol pyrophosphates has been illucidated. Two main classes of enzymes are involved in yeast and mammals: 5-kinases (encoded in mammals by |FRAME: HS10997|, |FRAME: HS00942| and |FRAME: HS08619|, and in yeast by KcsI) |CITS: [10574768][11502751]| and 1/3 kinases (encoded in mammals by |FRAME: HS09822| and |FRAME: HS07278| and in yeast by Vip1) |CITS:[17412958][18981179]|. All of these enzymes can phosphorylate |FRAME: MI-HEXAKISPHOSPHATE|, generating either |FRAME: 5-DIPHOSPHO-1D-MYO-INOSITOL-12346P| (in the case of IP6K) or a mixture of |FRAME: CPD-11700| and |FRAME: CPD-11937| (in the case of PPIP5K). These compounds, which contain a single pyrophosphate, are also known simply as InsP7. In addition, each enzyme class can continue to phosphorylate the product of the other enzyme class, resulting in a mixture of |FRAME: CPD-11938| and |FRAME: CPD-11939|, also known as InsP8 |CITS: [18981179]|. Another inositol pyrophosphate that is found in cells is |FRAME: DIPHOSPHO-1D-MYO-INOSITOL-TETRAKISPHOSPH|, whose exact stereomeric structure has not been defined yet. This compound is synthesized from |FRAME: CPD-1107| by the three IP6K |CITS: [10827188 ]|, as well as the |FRAME: HS07712-MONOMER| (|FRAME: HS07712|) |CITS: [11226235]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="SRI" />
    <Author name="caspi" />
  </Authors>
